<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Xia</h1><p class="st-meta">1038 CE – 1227 CE · capital Xingqing</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The empire of the Tangut people in north-west China, with its own script and Buddhist culture, destroyed by Genghis Khan in 1227.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tanguts</a> were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibeto-burman-languages">Tibeto-Burman</a> people whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> chiefs governed the Xiazhou region in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordos-plateau">Ordos</a> as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> governors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jiqian">Li Jiqian</a> broke with the Song in 982, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-deming">Li Deming</a> expanded west into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>, and in 1038 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yuanhao">Li Yuanhao</a> proclaimed himself emperor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ming-xia">Great Xia</a>, with his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yinchuan">Xingqing</a> (modern Yinchuan).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Western Xia combined a tribal military aristocracy with Chinese-style ministries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yuanhao">Li Yuanhao</a> introduced a distinctive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tangut</a> script in 1036, used for laws, histories and a huge translation of the Buddhist canon. The state controlled trade routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty</a> (1115-1234), extracting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> after 1044.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> attacked from 1205. After Western Xia refused to join the campaign against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> invaded in 1226; he died during the siege in 1227, and the capital fell soon after. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tangut</a> population was massacred or scattered.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/western-xia.jpg" alt="Western Xia" width="320" height="309"><figcaption>Image: Douglasfrankfort · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Song-Liao-Xixia-1111.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1038 CE – 1227 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xingqing</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Li Yuanhao (Emperor Jingzong), Li Renxiao (Emperor Renzong), Li Xian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tangut, Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China, Mongolia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xi Xia, Tangut Empire, Great Xia</dd></dl></aside></div>

